<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">O. Buse
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">R. Hind
</span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">August 24, 2026</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We prove packing stability for any closed symplectic manifold with rational cohomology class. This will rely on a general symplectic embedding result for ellipsoids which assumes only that there is no volume obstruction and that the domain is sufficiently thin relative to the target. We also obtain easily computable bounds for the Embedded Contact Homology capacities which are sufficient to imply the existence of some volume preserving embeddings in dimension <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We will study symplectic embeddings, that is, embeddings <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:(N,\sigma)\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:(N,\sigma)\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> between symplectic manifolds such that <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\omega=\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\omega=\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. As our domains will be ellipsoids or unions of balls in Euclidean space it is sometimes convenient to introduce the weaker notion <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N,\sigma)\hookrightarrow(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N,\sigma)\hookrightarrow(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> to mean that <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(N,\sigma):=(N,\lambda\sigma)\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(N,\sigma):=(N,\lambda\sigma)\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\lambda&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\lambda&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. We note that when <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a ball and <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is an ellipsoid or a disjoint union of balls in dimension <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N,\sigma)\hookrightarrow(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N,\sigma)\hookrightarrow(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> implies there exists a symplectic embedding <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathring{N},\sigma)\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>N</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathring{N},\sigma)\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> from the interior <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>N</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{N}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, Corollary <math id="S1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.6</annotation></semantics></math>. However no such results are known in higher dimension. In any case, we say that <math id="S1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N,\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N,\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fully fills</span> <math id="S1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{vol}(N,\sigma)=\mathrm{vol}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{vol}(N,\sigma)=\mathrm{vol}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N,\sigma)\hookrightarrow(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N,\sigma)\hookrightarrow(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our main theorem is an embedding result for ellipsoids. To state this, we denote by <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> the ellipsoid</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="E(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})=\left\{\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{\pi(x_{i}^{2}+y_{i}^{2})}{a_{i}}\leq 1\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})=\left\{\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{\pi(x_{i}^{2}+y_{i}^{2})}{a_{i}}\leq 1\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Ellipsoids inherit a symplectic structure from the standard form <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{0}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}dx_{i}\wedge dy_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{0}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}dx_{i}\wedge dy_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>. In our notation, the ball of capacity <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is written <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n}(c)=E(c,\dots,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n}(c)=E(c,\dots,c)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda E(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})=E(\lambda a_{1},\dots,\lambda a_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda E(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})=E(\lambda a_{1},\dots,\lambda a_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Any given ellipsoid is symplectomorphic to the same ellipsoid with its factors permuted. Unless otherwise stated, when describing an ellipsoid we will list the factors in increasing order.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a constant <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(b_{1},\dots,b_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(b_{1},\dots,b_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> such that if <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{a_{n}}{a_{1}}&gt;S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{a_{n}}{a_{1}}&gt;S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}\dots a_{n}\leq b_{1}\dots b_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}\dots a_{n}\leq b_{1}\dots b_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> there exists an embedding</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})\hookrightarrow E(b_{1},\dots,b_{n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})\hookrightarrow E(b_{1},\dots,b_{n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This will be established using a technique from Buse-Hind <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> which generates higher dimensional embeddings from lower dimensional ones, together with some new ellipsoid embeddings in dimension <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Combining with work of Biran <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> and Opshtein <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, we also have a full filling result valid for general rational symplectic manifolds. Recall that the manifold <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">rational</span> if <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]\in H^{2}(M,\mathbb{Q})\subset H^{2}(M,{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]\in H^{2}(M,\mathbb{Q})\subset H^{2}(M,{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{2n},\lambda\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{2n},\lambda\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>) is rational, then there exist a constant <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(M)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(M)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all ellipsoids <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{a_{n}}{a_{1}}&gt;S(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{a_{n}}{a_{1}}&gt;S(M)</annotation></semantics></math> we have a volume filling embedding <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})\hookrightarrow(M,\omega)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})\hookrightarrow(M,\omega).</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In the paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, section <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, Cieliebak, Hofer, Schlenk and Latschev define generalized symplectic capacities defined in terms of a fixed symplectic manifold <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> has dimension <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math> then we get a capacity on the space of <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math> dimensional ellipsoids defined by</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{(M,\omega)}E(a_{1},\cdots,a_{n}):=\inf\{c|E(a_{1},\cdots,a_{n})\hookrightarrow(M,c\omega)\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{(M,\omega)}E(a_{1},\cdots,a_{n}):=\inf\{c|E(a_{1},\cdots,a_{n})\hookrightarrow(M,c\omega)\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Our result can be interpreted as saying that <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{(M,\omega)}E(a_{1},\cdots,a_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{(M,\omega)}E(a_{1},\cdots,a_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with the normalized volume capacity</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{(M,\omega)}E(a_{1},\cdots,a_{n}):=\sqrt[n]{\frac{{\rm vol}(E(a_{1}\cdot\ldots\cdot a_{n}))}{{\rm vol}(M)}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mroot><mfrac><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mi>n</mi></mroot></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{(M,\omega)}E(a_{1},\cdots,a_{n}):=\sqrt[n]{\frac{{\rm vol}(E(a_{1}\cdot\ldots\cdot a_{n}))}{{\rm vol}(M)}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">whenever <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is rational and <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{a_{n}}{a_{1}}&gt;S(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{a_{n}}{a_{1}}&gt;S(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, whenever the ellipsoid is sufficiently thin.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The existence of an embedding between <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional ellipsoids is determined by the Embedded Contact Homology (ECH) capacities of M. Hutchings, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. Namely, there is a sequence of numbers <math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(a,b)(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(a,b)(k)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, associated to a pair <math id="S1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> and according to D. McDuff, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, we have <math id="S1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{1},a_{2})\hookrightarrow E(b_{1},b_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{1},a_{2})\hookrightarrow E(b_{1},b_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(a_{1},a_{2})(k)\leq\mathcal{N}(b_{1},b_{2})(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(a_{1},a_{2})(k)\leq\mathcal{N}(b_{1},b_{2})(k)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Although in principle this solves the problem of ellipsoid embeddings in dimension <math id="S1.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, it remains a difficult question to determine if a particular embedding exists since the <math id="S1.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(a,b)(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(a,b)(k)</annotation></semantics></math> are given by quite complicated combinatorial formulas, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. We will give quadratic bounds on a related piecewise linear function, see Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Proposition 2.5. ‣ 2.1 Description of ECH and some new estimates ‣ 2 Ellipsoid embeddings in dimension four ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>. One consequence is the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\geq\frac{(5\beta+16)^{2}}{16\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\geq\frac{(5\beta+16)^{2}}{16\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists a full filling <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(1,\alpha)\hookrightarrow\sqrt{\frac{\alpha}{\beta}}E(1,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msqrt><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mi>β</mi></mfrac></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(1,\alpha)\hookrightarrow\sqrt{\frac{\alpha}{\beta}}E(1,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Finally we discuss packing stability. The
<math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> packing number of a compact, <math id="S1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional, symplectic
manifold <math id="S1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}(M,\omega)=\frac{\sup_{c}{\rm Vol}(\sqcup_{k}B(c))}{{\rm Vol}(M,\omega)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub><mi>Vol</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊔</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}(M,\omega)=\frac{\sup_{c}{\rm Vol}(\sqcup_{k}B(c))}{{\rm Vol}(M,\omega)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p10.2" class="ltx_p">where the supremum is taken over all <math id="S1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> for which there exist a
symplectic embedding of <math id="S1.p10.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sqcup_{k}B(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⊔</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqcup_{k}B(c)</annotation></semantics></math>, the disjoint union of <math id="S1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> balls of capacity <math id="S1.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, into <math id="S1.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. Naturally,
<math id="S1.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}(M,\omega)\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}(M,\omega)\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. The identity <math id="S1.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}(M,\omega)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}(M,\omega)=1</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to saying that
<math id="S1.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> admits a full filling by <math id="S1.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> identical balls, otherwise we say that
there is a packing obstruction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The symplectic manifold <math id="S1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> has <span id="S1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">packing stability</span> if there exists an integer <math id="S1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{{\rm stab}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>stab</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{{\rm stab}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}(M,\omega)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}(M,\omega)=1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq N_{{\rm stab}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>stab</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq N_{{\rm stab}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{2n},\lambda\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{2n},\lambda\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>) is rational, then it has packing stability.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">This is due to P. Biran, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, in the case that <math id="S1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a closed four dimensional symplectic manifold with a rational cohomology class, and to Buse-Hind <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> when <math id="S1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with its Fubini-Study symplectic form.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Bounds on <math id="S1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{{\rm stab}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>stab</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{{\rm stab}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> can be derived from Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, although in specific examples we can obtain much sharper estimates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be equipped with its Fubini-Study symplectic form, and denote by <math id="S1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> a smooth hypersurface of degree <math id="S1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> with the induced symplectic form.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.5</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(i) <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{{\rm stab}}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})\leq\lceil(8\frac{1}{36})^{\frac{n}{2}}\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>stab</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⁤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>36</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{{\rm stab}}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})\leq\lceil(8\frac{1}{36})^{\frac{n}{2}}\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>. 
<br class="ltx_break">(ii) <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{{\rm stab}}(H^{n}_{d})\leq\lceil(\frac{25}{16}d+10d^{-\frac{(n-2)}{n}}+16d^{-\frac{2(n-1)}{n}})^{\frac{n}{2}}\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>stab</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>25</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{{\rm stab}}(H^{n}_{d})\leq\lceil(\frac{25}{16}d+10d^{-\frac{(n-2)}{n}}+16d^{-\frac{2(n-1)}{n}})^{\frac{n}{2}}\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Of course, statement <math id="S1.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i)</annotation></semantics></math> here is just a refined estimate of statement <math id="S1.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ii)</annotation></semantics></math> valid for hyperplanes and it improves the previous bounds provided in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p16.1" class="ltx_p">One can also consider packing with other symplectic manifolds, typically replacing the ball by other open subsets of <math id="S1.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{R}}^{2n},\omega_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{R}}^{2n},\omega_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>. Fixing a symplectic <math id="S1.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(D,\omega_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(D,\omega_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension <math id="S1.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math> we define the <math id="S1.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> packing number with respect to <math id="S1.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> of a <math id="S1.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional symplectic
manifold <math id="S1.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{D}_{k}(M,\omega)=\frac{\sup_{c}{\rm Vol}(\sqcup_{k}(D,c\omega_{0}))}{{\rm Vol}(M,\omega)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>D</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub><mi>Vol</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊔</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>ω</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{D}_{k}(M,\omega)=\frac{\sup_{c}{\rm Vol}(\sqcup_{k}(D,c\omega_{0}))}{{\rm Vol}(M,\omega)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p16.2" class="ltx_p">where the supremum is taken over all <math id="S1.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> for which there exist a
symplectic embedding of <math id="S1.p16.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sqcup_{k}(D,c\omega_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⊔</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqcup_{k}(D,c\omega_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S1.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly to the above, we say that <math id="S1.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> has packing stability with respect to <math id="S1.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(D,\omega_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(D,\omega_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S1.p16.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{D}_{k}(M,\omega)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>D</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{D}_{k}(M,\omega)=1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.p16.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently large. Taking <math id="S1.p16.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> to be an ellipsoid, we can generalize Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.6</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{2n},\lambda\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{2n},\lambda\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>) is rational, then it has packing stability with respect to any symplectic ellipsoid.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p17.1" class="ltx_p">We know of no manifolds which have packing stability with respect to other domains in <math id="S1.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>, for example polydisks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Outline of the paper.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p19.1" class="ltx_p">In section <math id="S1.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> we discuss the ECH capacities, deriving our quadratic bounds and in particular proving Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>. Section <math id="S1.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> gives the proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>. In section <math id="S1.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> we describe how to obtain Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> and packing stability, Theorems <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a>, from Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> together with a construction of E. Opshtein, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. In section <math id="S1.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> we compute some examples and prove Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acknowledgements.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p21.1" class="ltx_p">The first author would like to thank Dusa McDuff for suggesting Opshtein’s paper as a potential starting point towards adressing the packing problems in higher dimension, and Michal Misiurewicz and Rodrigo Pérez for several fruitful discussions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Ellipsoid embeddings in dimension four</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we consider the embedding capacity function</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\beta}(\alpha):=\inf\{c|E(1,\alpha)\hookrightarrow cE(1,\beta)\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\beta}(\alpha):=\inf\{c|E(1,\alpha)\hookrightarrow cE(1,\beta)\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">which is a natural extension of the function <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> considered by McDuff and Schlenk in the paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, see Theorem <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.1.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.1.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.1.2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We will show that <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\beta}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\beta}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with the normalized volume capacity of the ellipsoid <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(1,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(1,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> for sufficiently large values of <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Description of ECH and some new estimates</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The key ingredients for our study are results of Hutchings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> and McDuff <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> on Embedded Contact Homology which together give necessary and sufficient conditions for a four dimensional ellipsoid embedding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A four dimensional Liouville domain is a compact exact symplectic manifold <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> such that there exists a contact form on <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial X</annotation></semantics></math> which is a primitive of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega|_{\partial X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega|_{\partial X}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hutchings associates to such <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> an increasing sequence of real numbers <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> called the ECH capacities. The term capacity is justified by the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Hutchings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>) 
Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X^{\prime},\omega^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X^{\prime},\omega^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> be two Liouville domains as above.
If there exist a symplectic embedding <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:(X,\omega)\longrightarrow(\mathring{X}^{\prime},\omega^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:(X,\omega)\longrightarrow(\mathring{X}^{\prime},\omega^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}(X,\omega)\leq c_{k}(X^{\prime},\omega^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(X,\omega)\leq c_{k}(X^{\prime},\omega^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> for all integers <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the same paper, Hutchings proceeds to compute the capacities of ellipsoids <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;a\leq b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;a\leq b</annotation></semantics></math> consider the sequence
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(a,b)(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(a,b)(k)</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by arranging in increasing order,
with repetitions, all the numbers of the type <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\ell+bp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\ell+bp</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell,p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell,p</annotation></semantics></math>
any nonnegative integers. Then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}(E(a,b))=\mathcal{N}(a,b)(k-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(E(a,b))=\mathcal{N}(a,b)(k-1)</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Following this, McDuff showed that the necessary condition for ellipsoid embeddings coming from Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 2.1. ‣ 2.1 Description of ECH and some new estimates ‣ 2 Ellipsoid embeddings in dimension four ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> is also sufficient:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(McDuff <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>) There exists a symplectic embedding
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\;\;\mathring{E}(a,b)\longrightarrow E(a^{\prime},b^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\;\;\mathring{E}(a,b)\longrightarrow E(a^{\prime},b^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(a,b)(k)\leq\mathcal{N}(a^{\prime},b^{\prime})(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(a,b)(k)\leq\mathcal{N}(a^{\prime},b^{\prime})(k)</annotation></semantics></math> for all natural numbers <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Comparing Theorems <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 2.1. ‣ 2.1 Description of ECH and some new estimates ‣ 2 Ellipsoid embeddings in dimension four ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> and <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 2.3. ‣ 2.1 Description of ECH and some new estimates ‣ 2 Ellipsoid embeddings in dimension four ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> we remark that <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{E}(a,b)\to E(a^{\prime},b^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{E}(a,b)\to E(a^{\prime},b^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b)\to\mathring{E}(\lambda a^{\prime},\lambda b^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b)\to\mathring{E}(\lambda a^{\prime},\lambda b^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> Remark 1.3.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We will refer to <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(a,b)(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(a,b)(x)</annotation></semantics></math> as being the piecewise linear function built by joining by line segments the points of <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(a,b)(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(a,b)(k)</annotation></semantics></math>. It is clearly sufficient to compare two such piecewise functions and moreover, if one defines, for any <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(a,b)(y)=\sup\{k|\mathcal{N}(a,b)(k)\leq y\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(a,b)(y)=\sup\{k|\mathcal{N}(a,b)(k)\leq y\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">then we have</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(see Hutchings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, Bauer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>) <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b)\hookrightarrow E(a^{\prime},b^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b)\hookrightarrow E(a^{\prime},b^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>
if and only if</span></p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(a,b)(y)\geq R(a^{\prime},b^{\prime})(y)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(a,b)(y)\geq R(a^{\prime},b^{\prime})(y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for all <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">To prove theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> by using this corollary, we will first need the following estimates:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math> we have that</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{y^{2}}{2ab}+\frac{y}{2a}\leq R_{a,b}(y)\leq\frac{y^{2}}{2ab}+\frac{y}{2a}+\frac{y}{b}+\frac{b}{8a}+1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>y</mi><mi>b</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{y^{2}}{2ab}+\frac{y}{2a}\leq R_{a,b}(y)\leq\frac{y^{2}}{2ab}+\frac{y}{2a}+\frac{y}{b}+\frac{b}{8a}+1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">As pointed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{a,b}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{a,b}(y)</annotation></semantics></math> is interpreted geometrically as the number
of non-negative integer vectors <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math> in the closed triangle bounded by
<math id="S2.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and the diagonal <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="am+bn=y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">am+bn=y</annotation></semantics></math>. Equivalently, this represents the
area of the union of unit squares with lower-left corners <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S2.SS1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="am+bn\leq y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">am+bn\leq y</annotation></semantics></math>. The triangular area <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{y^{2}}{2ab}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{y^{2}}{2ab}</annotation></semantics></math> under the diagonal is
then an obvious lower estimate for <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{a,b}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{a,b}(y)</annotation></semantics></math>, but it omits the sizable
area of the staircase-shaped region <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> above the diagonal. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{y/b\}=\frac{y}{b}-\lfloor y/b\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>y</mi><mi>b</mi></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{y/b\}=\frac{y}{b}-\lfloor y/b\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> denote the fractional part of <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{y}{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>y</mi><mi>b</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{y}{b}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="1112.1149v1/pack1.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="width:39.949em; height:17.264em;aspect-ratio:663/287;" width="663" height="287" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Triangles <math id="S2.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and the region <math id="S2.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">To estimate the area of <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}=(0,y/b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}=(0,y/b)</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1},\ldots,p_{\lceil y/b\rceil}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1},\ldots,p_{\lceil y/b\rceil}</annotation></semantics></math> be the points of integer height along the diagonal,
indexed from left to right. For every <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq k\leq\lceil y/b\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq k\leq\lceil y/b\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>,
let <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be the triangle formed by <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the point one unit
above <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Every triangle <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}\subset S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}\subset S</annotation></semantics></math> rests atop the diagonal, these are the darkest triangles in Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Proof. ‣ 2.1 Description of ECH and some new estimates ‣ 2 Ellipsoid embeddings in dimension four ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. They all
have (vertical) base of length 1, and their (horizontal) heights add up to
<math id="S2.SS1.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y/a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y/a</annotation></semantics></math>; thus, the total area of the triangles is <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y/2a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y/2a</annotation></semantics></math>, and</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{y^{2}}{2ab}+\frac{y}{2a}\leq R_{a,b}(y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{y^{2}}{2ab}+\frac{y}{2a}\leq R_{a,b}(y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the upper estimate, add unit squares to the right of <math id="S2.SS1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1},\ldots,T_{\lceil y/b\rceil}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1},\ldots,T_{\lceil y/b\rceil}</annotation></semantics></math>, see Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Proof. ‣ 2.1 Description of ECH and some new estimates ‣ 2 Ellipsoid embeddings in dimension four ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. These have total area <math id="S2.SS1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lceil y/b\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lceil y/b\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>, and cover the remainder of
<math id="S2.SS1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, except (sometimes) for a thin triangle above <math id="S2.SS1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> sitting at the top left of Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Proof. ‣ 2.1 Description of ECH and some new estimates ‣ 2 Ellipsoid embeddings in dimension four ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
The (vertical) base of this triangle has length <math id="S2.SS1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\{y/b\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\{y/b\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Its (horizontal) height
is the first coordinate of <math id="S2.SS1.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, <math id="S2.SS1.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{b}{a}\{y/b\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{b}{a}\{y/b\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore we get</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{a,b}(y)\leq\frac{y^{2}}{2ab}+\frac{y}{2a}+\lceil\frac{y}{b}\rceil+\frac{b}{2a}\{y/b\}(1-\{y/b\})\leq\frac{y^{2}}{2ab}+\frac{y}{2a}+\frac{y}{b}+\frac{b}{8a}+1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mfrac><mi>y</mi><mi>b</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>y</mi><mi>b</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{a,b}(y)\leq\frac{y^{2}}{2ab}+\frac{y}{2a}+\lceil\frac{y}{b}\rceil+\frac{b}{2a}\{y/b\}(1-\{y/b\})\leq\frac{y^{2}}{2ab}+\frac{y}{2a}+\frac{y}{b}+\frac{b}{8a}+1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">where for the final inequality we note that <math id="S2.SS1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{y/b\}(1-\{y/b\})\leq\frac{1}{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{y/b\}(1-\{y/b\})\leq\frac{1}{4}</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S2.SS1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\{y/b\}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\{y/b\}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="1112.1149v1/pack2.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="width:25.796em; height:14.655em;aspect-ratio:428/243;" width="428" height="243" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Added squares covering <math id="S2.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>A volume filing ellipsoid embedding in dimension four</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We can now give a proof of theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let us call <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c:=\sqrt{\frac{\alpha}{\beta}}&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>:=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mi>β</mi></mfrac></msqrt><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c:=\sqrt{\frac{\alpha}{\beta}}&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> by hypothesis. Assuming <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\geq\frac{(5\beta+16)^{2}}{16\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\geq\frac{(5\beta+16)^{2}}{16\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> it is required to show that we have a volume filling embedding <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(1,\alpha)\hookrightarrow E(c,c\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(1,\alpha)\hookrightarrow E(c,c\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>. By Corollary <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Corollary 2.4. ‣ 2.1 Description of ECH and some new estimates ‣ 2 Ellipsoid embeddings in dimension four ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a> this is equivalent to showing</span></p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1,\alpha}(y)\geq R_{c,c\beta}(y)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1,\alpha}(y)\geq R_{c,c\beta}(y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">for all <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">As <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;\beta</annotation></semantics></math> it is easy to see that for <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq y\leq c\lfloor\beta\rfloor&lt;\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq y\leq c\lfloor\beta\rfloor&lt;\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1,\alpha}(y)=\lfloor y\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1,\alpha}(y)=\lfloor y\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{c,cb}(y)=\lfloor\frac{y}{c}\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{c,cb}(y)=\lfloor\frac{y}{c}\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore
the inequality (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In Proof. ‣ 2.2 A volume filing ellipsoid embedding in dimension four ‣ 2 Ellipsoid embeddings in dimension four ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) holds for values of <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\leq c\lfloor\beta\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\leq c\lfloor\beta\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>. Our hypothesis also implies that <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor\beta\rfloor+1\leq\lfloor\alpha\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor\beta\rfloor+1\leq\lfloor\alpha\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> and so the graphs of <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(1,\alpha)(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(1,\alpha)(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(c,c\beta)(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(c,c\beta)(x)</annotation></semantics></math> are as shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Proof. ‣ 2.2 A volume filing ellipsoid embedding in dimension four ‣ 2 Ellipsoid embeddings in dimension four ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> for small values of <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence we can also observe directly that inequality (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In Proof. ‣ 2.2 A volume filing ellipsoid embedding in dimension four ‣ 2 Ellipsoid embeddings in dimension four ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) holds in the range <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\lfloor\beta\rfloor\leq y\leq c\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\lfloor\beta\rfloor\leq y\leq c\beta</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="1112.1149v1/twoParabolas.png" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="width:24.692em; height:19.674em;aspect-ratio:410/327;" width="410" height="327" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>graphs of the ECH capacity functions</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">It remains to show that (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In Proof. ‣ 2.2 A volume filing ellipsoid embedding in dimension four ‣ 2 Ellipsoid embeddings in dimension four ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) holds for the remaining values <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\geq c\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\geq c\beta</annotation></semantics></math>.
Using the inequality (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In Proposition 2.5. ‣ 2.1 Description of ECH and some new estimates ‣ 2 Ellipsoid embeddings in dimension four ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) from Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Proposition 2.5. ‣ 2.1 Description of ECH and some new estimates ‣ 2 Ellipsoid embeddings in dimension four ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a> twice, once for <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1,\alpha}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1,\alpha}(y)</annotation></semantics></math> and again for <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{c,c\beta}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{c,c\beta}(y)</annotation></semantics></math>,
it is sufficient to show</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{y^{2}}{2\alpha}+\frac{y}{2}\geq\frac{y^{2}}{2c^{2}\beta}+\frac{y}{2c}+\frac{y}{c\beta}+\frac{c\beta}{8c}+1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{y^{2}}{2\alpha}+\frac{y}{2}\geq\frac{y^{2}}{2c^{2}\beta}+\frac{y}{2c}+\frac{y}{c\beta}+\frac{c\beta}{8c}+1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">for <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\geq c\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\geq c\beta</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the fact that <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=c^{2}\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=c^{2}\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, this reduces to showing that</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{y}{2}(\frac{c\beta-\beta-2}{c\beta})\geq\frac{\beta}{8}+1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mn>8</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{y}{2}(\frac{c\beta-\beta-2}{c\beta})\geq\frac{\beta}{8}+1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">for all <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\geq c\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\geq c\beta</annotation></semantics></math>. But this is equivalent to our hypothesis
<math id="S2.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\geq\frac{(5\beta+16)^{2}}{16\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\geq\frac{(5\beta+16)^{2}}{16\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Volume filling embeddings</h2>

<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Define a constant <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\frac{1}{n-1}}=\frac{2^{n+6}}{3}20^{\frac{n-2}{2}}\max_{k}20^{\frac{k(k-1)}{2}}\frac{b_{1}\dots b_{n}}{b_{k}^{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>20</mn><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>20</mn><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\frac{1}{n-1}}=\frac{2^{n+6}}{3}20^{\frac{n-2}{2}}\max_{k}20^{\frac{k(k-1)}{2}}\frac{b_{1}\dots b_{n}}{b_{k}^{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{a_{n}}{a_{1}}&gt;S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{a_{n}}{a_{1}}&gt;S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}\dots a_{n}=b_{1}\dots b_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}\dots a_{n}=b_{1}\dots b_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a volume preserving embedding</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})\hookrightarrow E(b_{1},\dots,b_{n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})\hookrightarrow E(b_{1},\dots,b_{n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We will apply a series of volume preserving embeddings to the ellipsoid <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. The factors of our ellipsoids will always be in increasing order. First recall our key tools.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(x)=\frac{(5x+16)^{2}}{16x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(x)=\frac{(5x+16)^{2}}{16x}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is decreasing on the interval <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{1\leq x\leq\frac{16}{5}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>16</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1\leq x\leq\frac{16}{5}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, increasing on <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{x\geq\frac{16}{5}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mn>16</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x\geq\frac{16}{5}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(\frac{16}{5})=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mn>16</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(\frac{16}{5})=20</annotation></semantics></math>. Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> gives the following.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{b}{a}&gt;m(\frac{d}{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{b}{a}&gt;m(\frac{d}{c})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ab=cd" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ab=cd</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b)\hookrightarrow E(c,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b)\hookrightarrow E(c,d)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{b}{a}&gt;\frac{2^{7}}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>7</mn></msup><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{b}{a}&gt;\frac{2^{7}}{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2a&lt;d&lt;\sqrt{ab}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2a&lt;d&lt;\sqrt{ab}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b)\hookrightarrow E(d,\frac{ab}{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b)\hookrightarrow E(d,\frac{ab}{d})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">The upper bound on <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> guarantees that the factors of our image ellipsoid are in increasing order. Therefore by Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 3.2. ‣ Proof. ‣ 3 Volume filling embeddings ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> we just need to check that <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{b}{a}&gt;m(\frac{ab}{d^{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{b}{a}&gt;m(\frac{ab}{d^{2}})</annotation></semantics></math>. We compute</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{b}{a}-m(\frac{ab}{d^{2}})=\frac{b}{a}-\frac{25}{16}\frac{ab}{d^{2}}-10-\frac{16d^{2}}{ab}\geq\frac{b}{a}-\frac{25}{16}\frac{b}{4a}-26=\frac{39b}{64a}-26." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>25</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>25</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>26</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>39</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>26</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{b}{a}-m(\frac{ab}{d^{2}})=\frac{b}{a}-\frac{25}{16}\frac{ab}{d^{2}}-10-\frac{16d^{2}}{ab}\geq\frac{b}{a}-\frac{25}{16}\frac{b}{4a}-26=\frac{39b}{64a}-26.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">This last term is positive as required given our hypothesis.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">We will also utilize Proposition <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.1</annotation></semantics></math> from Buse-Hind <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">]</span></cite><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b)\hookrightarrow E(c,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b)\hookrightarrow E(c,d)</annotation></semantics></math> then</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a,b,a_{3},\dots,a_{n})\hookrightarrow E(c,d,a_{3},\dots,a_{n})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a,b,a_{3},\dots,a_{n})\hookrightarrow E(c,d,a_{3},\dots,a_{n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for any <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{3},\dots,a_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{3},\dots,a_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Note here that as the order of the factors is irrelevant analogous statements hold for all other pairs of factors.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">We start with the following lemma.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a volume preserving embedding <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})\hookrightarrow E(a^{\prime}_{1},\dots,a^{\prime}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})\hookrightarrow E(a^{\prime}_{1},\dots,a^{\prime}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i).</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\prime}_{n}=a_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\prime}_{n}=a_{n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii).</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{a^{\prime}_{k}}{a^{\prime}_{k-1}}&lt;20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{a^{\prime}_{k}}{a^{\prime}_{k-1}}&lt;20</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq k\leq n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq k\leq n-1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii).</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{a^{\prime}_{n}}{a^{\prime}_{k}}&gt;20^{-\frac{n-2}{2}}S^{1/{n-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msup><mn>20</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{a^{\prime}_{n}}{a^{\prime}_{k}}&gt;20^{-\frac{n-2}{2}}S^{1/{n-1}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq k\leq n-1." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq k\leq n-1.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">We perform a sequence of embeddings fixing throughout the largest factor <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> of the ellipsoid.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 3.2. ‣ Proof. ‣ 3 Volume filling embeddings ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> implies that if <math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{a_{k}}{a_{k-1}}&gt;20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{a_{k}}{a_{k-1}}&gt;20</annotation></semantics></math> then there exists an embedding <math id="S3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{k-1},a_{k})\hookrightarrow\sqrt{\frac{5a_{k-1}a_{k}}{16}}E(1,\frac{16}{5})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mn>16</mn></mfrac></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>16</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{k-1},a_{k})\hookrightarrow\sqrt{\frac{5a_{k-1}a_{k}}{16}}E(1,\frac{16}{5})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">We will apply the embedding of Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Proposition 3.4. ‣ Proof. ‣ 3 Volume filling embeddings ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> induced by this four dimensional embedding to any consecutive pair of factors <math id="S3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{k-1},a_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{k-1},a_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="S3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math> and whose ratio is greater than <math id="S3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math>. After such an operation the product of the first <math id="S3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math> factors will increase by a factor of at least <math id="S3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\frac{100}{16}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mfrac><mn>100</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\frac{100}{16}}</annotation></semantics></math>, but the product of the first <math id="S3.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> factors is fixed throughout. Thus after a finite number of steps we arrive at an ellipsoid <math id="S3.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a^{\prime}_{1},\dots,a^{\prime}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a^{\prime}_{1},\dots,a^{\prime}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying condition <math id="S3.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ii)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">For condition <math id="S3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(iii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(iii)</annotation></semantics></math> we estimate</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\frac{a^{\prime}_{n}}{a^{\prime}_{k}}\right)^{n-1}\geq\left(\frac{a^{\prime}_{n}}{a^{\prime}_{n-1}}\right)^{n-1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≥</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\frac{a^{\prime}_{n}}{a^{\prime}_{k}}\right)^{n-1}\geq\left(\frac{a^{\prime}_{n}}{a^{\prime}_{n-1}}\right)^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq\frac{(a^{\prime}_{n})^{n-1}}{20^{(n-1)(n-2)/2}}\cdot\frac{20\cdot 20^{2}\cdot\ldots\cdot 20^{n-2}}{(a^{\prime}_{n-1})^{n-1}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mn>20</mn><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>20</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>20</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq\frac{(a^{\prime}_{n})^{n-1}}{20^{(n-1)(n-2)/2}}\cdot\frac{20\cdot 20^{2}\cdot\ldots\cdot 20^{n-2}}{(a^{\prime}_{n-1})^{n-1}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq\frac{(a^{\prime}_{n})^{n-1}}{20^{(n-1)(n-2)/2}\cdot a^{\prime}_{n-1}\cdot a^{\prime}_{n-2}\cdot\ldots\cdot a^{\prime}_{1}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><msup><mn>20</mn><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq\frac{(a^{\prime}_{n})^{n-1}}{20^{(n-1)(n-2)/2}\cdot a^{\prime}_{n-1}\cdot a^{\prime}_{n-2}\cdot\ldots\cdot a^{\prime}_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p11.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p11.2.1" class="ltx_text">using property <math id="S3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ii)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now, as all our embeddings are volume preserving and fix <math id="S3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the product of the first <math id="S3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> terms is also preserved under our sequence of embeddings. Thus we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\frac{a^{\prime}_{n}}{a^{\prime}_{k}}\right)^{n-1}\geq\frac{a_{n}^{n-1}}{20^{(n-1)(n-2)/2}\cdot a_{n-1}\cdot a_{n-2}\cdot\ldots\cdot a_{1}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msup><mn>20</mn><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\frac{a^{\prime}_{n}}{a^{\prime}_{k}}\right)^{n-1}\geq\frac{a_{n}^{n-1}}{20^{(n-1)(n-2)/2}\cdot a_{n-1}\cdot a_{n-2}\cdot\ldots\cdot a_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 20^{\frac{-(n-1)(n-2)}{2}}S," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mn>20</mn><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 20^{\frac{-(n-1)(n-2)}{2}}S,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p12.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p12.2.1" class="ltx_text">where for the final inequality we used simply that <math id="S3.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{k}\leq a_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{k}\leq a_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math> and that by hypothesis <math id="S3.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}&gt;Sa_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}&gt;Sa_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Our lemma follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now we drop the primes from the ellipsoid resulting from Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 3.5. ‣ Proof. ‣ 3 Volume filling embeddings ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> and write simply <math id="S3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> for our new range.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{k}&gt;2a_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{k}&gt;2a_{k}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq k\leq n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq k\leq n-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">For any <math id="S3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1=\frac{a_{1}\ldots a_{n}}{b_{1}\ldots b_{n}}\geq\frac{a_{1}\ldots a_{k-1}a_{k}^{n-k}a_{n}}{b_{1}\ldots b_{n}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1=\frac{a_{1}\ldots a_{n}}{b_{1}\ldots b_{n}}\geq\frac{a_{1}\ldots a_{k-1}a_{k}^{n-k}a_{n}}{b_{1}\ldots b_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq\frac{a_{k}^{n-k}}{b_{1}\ldots b_{n}}\frac{a_{k}}{20^{k-1}}\ldots\frac{a_{k}}{20}20^{-\frac{n-2}{2}}S^{1/{n-1}}a_{k}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mfrac><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mn>20</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mn>20</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>20</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq\frac{a_{k}^{n-k}}{b_{1}\ldots b_{n}}\frac{a_{k}}{20^{k-1}}\ldots\frac{a_{k}}{20}20^{-\frac{n-2}{2}}S^{1/{n-1}}a_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p14.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p14.2.1" class="ltx_text">using properties <math id="S3.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ii)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(iii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(iii)</annotation></semantics></math> from Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 3.5. ‣ Proof. ‣ 3 Volume filling embeddings ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a></span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=\left(\frac{a_{k}}{b_{k}}\right)^{n}\frac{20^{-\frac{n-2}{2}}S^{1/{n-1}}(b_{k})^{n}}{20^{k(k-1)/2}b_{1}\dots b_{n}}\geq\left(\frac{a_{k}}{b_{k}}\right)^{n}2^{n}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mn>20</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>20</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=\left(\frac{a_{k}}{b_{k}}\right)^{n}\frac{20^{-\frac{n-2}{2}}S^{1/{n-1}}(b_{k})^{n}}{20^{k(k-1)/2}b_{1}\dots b_{n}}\geq\left(\frac{a_{k}}{b_{k}}\right)^{n}2^{n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p14.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p14.3.1" class="ltx_text">and the Lemma follows.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now we complete the proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3 Volume filling embeddings ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. We will proceed by induction on <math id="S3.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,\dots,n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,\dots,n-1</annotation></semantics></math>. After the <math id="S3.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="kth" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">kth</annotation></semantics></math> stage we will have found a volume preserving embedding into an ellipsoid of the form <math id="S3.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(b_{1},\dots,b_{k},a_{k+1},\dots,a_{n-1},a_{n,k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(b_{1},\dots,b_{k},a_{k+1},\dots,a_{n-1},a_{n,k})</annotation></semantics></math> where the <math id="S3.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are the factors of our latest ellipsoid above and <math id="S3.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n,k}</annotation></semantics></math> will satisfy <math id="S3.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{a_{n,k}}{a_{n-1}}&gt;\frac{M}{2^{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><mi>M</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{a_{n,k}}{a_{n-1}}&gt;\frac{M}{2^{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here we define <math id="S3.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=20^{-\frac{n-2}{2}}S^{1/{n-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>20</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=20^{-\frac{n-2}{2}}S^{1/{n-1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, so by Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 3.5. ‣ Proof. ‣ 3 Volume filling embeddings ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> we have <math id="S3.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{a_{n}}{a_{n-1}}&gt;M." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{a_{n}}{a_{n-1}}&gt;M.</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">For our inductive step, we claim that if <math id="S3.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{a_{n,k-1}}{a_{n-1}}&gt;\frac{M}{2^{k-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><mi>M</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{a_{n,k-1}}{a_{n-1}}&gt;\frac{M}{2^{k-1}}</annotation></semantics></math> then there exists an embedding</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{k},a_{n,k-1})\hookrightarrow E(b_{k},\frac{a_{n,k-1}a_{k}}{b_{k}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{k},a_{n,k-1})\hookrightarrow E(b_{k},\frac{a_{n,k-1}a_{k}}{b_{k}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p17.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p17.2.1" class="ltx_text">We can then set <math id="S3.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n,k}=\frac{a_{n,k-1}a_{k}}{b_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n,k}=\frac{a_{n,k-1}a_{k}}{b_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> and provided <math id="S3.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;n-1</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S3.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{a_{n,k}}{a_{n-1}}&gt;\frac{M}{2^{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><mi>M</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{a_{n,k}}{a_{n-1}}&gt;\frac{M}{2^{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> by Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 3.6. ‣ Proof. ‣ 3 Volume filling embeddings ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>. Thus by repeated applications of Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Proposition 3.4. ‣ Proof. ‣ 3 Volume filling embeddings ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> we can conclude by induction. After the <math id="S3.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math>st step we will automatically have <math id="S3.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n,n-1}=b_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n,n-1}=b_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> by the hypothesis <math id="S3.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}\dots a_{n}=b_{1}\dots b_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}\dots a_{n}=b_{1}\dots b_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and the fact that all of our embeddings are volume preserving.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">To justify the claim, we will apply Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Corollary 3.3. ‣ Proof. ‣ 3 Volume filling embeddings ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> with <math id="S3.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=a_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=a_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=a_{n,k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=a_{n,k-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=b_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=b_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. There are three conditions to check. First</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{a}{b}=\frac{a_{n,k-1}}{a_{k}}\geq\frac{a_{n,k-1}}{a_{n-1}}&gt;\frac{M}{2^{k-1}}=20^{-\frac{n-2}{2}}2^{-k+1}S^{1/{n-1}}&gt;\frac{2^{7}}{3}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><mi>M</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>20</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>7</mn></msup><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{a}{b}=\frac{a_{n,k-1}}{a_{k}}\geq\frac{a_{n,k-1}}{a_{n-1}}&gt;\frac{M}{2^{k-1}}=20^{-\frac{n-2}{2}}2^{-k+1}S^{1/{n-1}}&gt;\frac{2^{7}}{3}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p18.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p18.2.1" class="ltx_text">by our assumption on <math id="S3.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Secondly, <math id="S3.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=b_{k}&lt;2a_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=b_{k}&lt;2a_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> by Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 3.6. ‣ Proof. ‣ 3 Volume filling embeddings ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>. Finally, <math id="S3.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{2}=b_{k}^{2}\leq b_{k}b_{n}&lt;a_{k}a_{n,k-1}=ab" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{2}=b_{k}^{2}\leq b_{k}b_{n}&lt;a_{k}a_{n,k-1}=ab</annotation></semantics></math> as our ellipsoids have equal volume but <math id="S3.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{k}&gt;a_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{k}&gt;a_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Full filling by an ellipsoid</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we present Opshtein’s result, which is a refinement of Biran’s polarization theorem, that any rational symplectic manifold can be fully filled by an ellipsoid, that is, we prove the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> be rational. Then there exist <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1},\dots,b_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1},\dots,b_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> such that there exists a full filling <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(b_{1},\dots,b_{n})\hookrightarrow(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(b_{1},\dots,b_{n})\hookrightarrow(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 ([] []). ‣ 4 Full filling by an ellipsoid ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> we can prove Theorems <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a>. Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> is also a direct application of Theorems <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 ([] []). ‣ 4 Full filling by an ellipsoid ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>.</span> Given <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> rational, we consider the fully filling ellipsoid <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(b_{1},\dots,b_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(b_{1},\dots,b_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> given by Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 ([] []). ‣ 4 Full filling by an ellipsoid ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>. By Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> there exists a constant <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(b_{1},\dots,b_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(b_{1},\dots,b_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> such that there exists a full filling <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma E(1,\dots,1,k)\hookrightarrow E(b_{1},\dots,b_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma E(1,\dots,1,k)\hookrightarrow E(b_{1},\dots,b_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq S</annotation></semantics></math>. But from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, Lemma <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.1</annotation></semantics></math> there also exists a full filling</p>
<table id="S4.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sqcup_{k}B(1)\hookrightarrow E\big(1^{\times(n-1)},k\big)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⊔</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqcup_{k}B(1)\hookrightarrow E\big(1^{\times(n-1)},k\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">for any <math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Putting the two maps together, we obtain a full filling of <math id="S4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(b_{1},\dots,b_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(b_{1},\dots,b_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence <math id="S4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, by <math id="S4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> balls of capacity <math id="S4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. This means that <math id="S4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> has packing stability as required. <span id="S4.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_italic">∎</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a>.</span> Fix an ellipsoid <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=E(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=E(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. We follow an identical line of argument to the proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> above, letting <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(b_{1},\dots,b_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(b_{1},\dots,b_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> be the filling ellipsoid for <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> from Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 ([] []). ‣ 4 Full filling by an ellipsoid ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a full filling</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sqcup_{k}E\big(a_{1},\dots,a_{n}\big)\hookrightarrow E\big(a_{1},\dots,a_{n-1},ka_{n}\big)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⊔</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqcup_{k}E\big(a_{1},\dots,a_{n}\big)\hookrightarrow E\big(a_{1},\dots,a_{n-1},ka_{n}\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for any <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Define two subsets of <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows.</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="T=\{0\leq x_{1},\dots,x_{n},0\leq y_{1},\dots,y_{n}\leq 1|\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{x_{i}}{a_{i}}\leq 1\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\{0\leq x_{1},\dots,x_{n},0\leq y_{1},\dots,y_{n}\leq 1|\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{x_{i}}{a_{i}}\leq 1\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">and</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="U=\{0\leq x_{1},\dots,x_{n},0\leq y_{1},\dots,y_{n-1}\leq 1,0\leq y_{n}\leq k|\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{x_{i}}{a_{i}}\leq 1\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=\{0\leq x_{1},\dots,x_{n},0\leq y_{1},\dots,y_{n-1}\leq 1,0\leq y_{n}\leq k|\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{x_{i}}{a_{i}}\leq 1\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">Lemma <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.3.1(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.3.1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.3.1(i)</annotation></semantics></math> from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> gives an embedding <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})\hookrightarrow T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(a_{1},\dots,a_{n})\hookrightarrow T</annotation></semantics></math> and hence an embedding</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sqcup_{k}E\big(a_{1},\dots,a_{n}\big)\hookrightarrow U." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⊔</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqcup_{k}E\big(a_{1},\dots,a_{n}\big)\hookrightarrow U.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.4.1" class="ltx_text">Lemma <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.3.1(ii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.3.1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.3.1(ii)</annotation></semantics></math> implies that there exists an embedding <math id="S4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\hookrightarrow E(a_{1},\dots,ka_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\hookrightarrow E(a_{1},\dots,ka_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Composing these two our lemma follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">But by Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> there exists a constant <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(b_{1},\dots,b_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(b_{1},\dots,b_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> such that there exists a full filling <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma E(1,\dots,1,k)\hookrightarrow E(b_{1},\dots,b_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma E(1,\dots,1,k)\hookrightarrow E(b_{1},\dots,b_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq S</annotation></semantics></math>. This map and the embedding from our lemma together give packing stability for <math id="S4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_italic">∎</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Now we proceed to prove Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 ([] []). ‣ 4 Full filling by an ellipsoid ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> by induction on the dimension of the manifold <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Observe that Moser’s theorem implies a surface can be fully filled by any number of balls, so we assume that the theorem holds for all manifolds of dimension <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> be rational of dimension <math id="S4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Using the rationality assumption, a theorem of S. K. Donaldson, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, says that <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> can be polarized in the sense that there exists a symplectic codimension <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> submanifold <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> which is Poincaré dual to <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> for a suitable multiple <math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=\omega|_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=\omega|_{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SDB(N,\tau,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SDB(N,\tau,m)</annotation></semantics></math> be a standard symplectic disk bundle over <math id="S4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. This is a symplectic manifold whose underlying smooth manifold is the disk bundle over <math id="S4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> with Euler class <math id="S4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[m\tau]\in H^{2}(N,{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[m\tau]\in H^{2}(N,{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> and which has a symplectic form restricting to <math id="S4.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> and with fibers of area <math id="S4.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. As a symplectic manifold, <math id="S4.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SDB(N,\tau,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SDB(N,\tau,m)</annotation></semantics></math> is well defined up to isotopy. For more details see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, section <math id="S4.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, section <math id="S4.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. Given the above, we can now state the Biran decomposition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Theorem <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a full filling <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SDB(N,\tau,m)\hookrightarrow(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SDB(N,\tau,m)\hookrightarrow(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">By our induction hypothesis, there exits <math id="S4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1},\dots,b_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1},\dots,b_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and a full filling <math id="S4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(b_{1},\dots,b_{n-1})\hookrightarrow(N,\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(b_{1},\dots,b_{n-1})\hookrightarrow(N,\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>. Now we apply the construction of Opshtein.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Lemma <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.1</annotation></semantics></math>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A symplectic embedding <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(b_{1},\dots,b_{n-1})\hookrightarrow(N,\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(b_{1},\dots,b_{n-1})\hookrightarrow(N,\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> can be extended to a symplectic embedding <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(b_{1},\dots,b_{n-1},\frac{1}{m})\hookrightarrow SDB(N,\tau,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(b_{1},\dots,b_{n-1},\frac{1}{m})\hookrightarrow SDB(N,\tau,m)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p11.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(b_{1},\dots,b_{n-1})\hookrightarrow(N,\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(b_{1},\dots,b_{n-1})\hookrightarrow(N,\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> is a full filling then the embedding <math id="S4.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(b_{1},\dots,b_{n-1},\frac{1}{m})\hookrightarrow SDB(N,\tau,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(b_{1},\dots,b_{n-1},\frac{1}{m})\hookrightarrow SDB(N,\tau,m)</annotation></semantics></math> of Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 4.4 (Lemma 2.1, []). ‣ 4 Full filling by an ellipsoid ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> is also a full filling. Thus, combining Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 4.3 (Theorem 1, [],[]). ‣ 4 Full filling by an ellipsoid ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> and Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 4.4 (Lemma 2.1, []). ‣ 4 Full filling by an ellipsoid ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> we have a full filling <math id="S4.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(b_{1},\dots,b_{n-1},\frac{1}{m})\hookrightarrow(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(b_{1},\dots,b_{n-1},\frac{1}{m})\hookrightarrow(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> and we conclude the proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 ([] []). ‣ 4 Full filling by an ellipsoid ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> by induction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Examples</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span><math id="S5.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Complex projective space <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is fully filled by a ball. Recall also that <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(1,\dots,1,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(1,\dots,1,k)</annotation></semantics></math> can be fully filled by <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> balls, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, Lemma <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.1</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore we can establish our stability bound for <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> by producing an embedding <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(1,\dots,1,k)\hookrightarrow B(k^{\frac{1}{n}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(1,\dots,1,k)\hookrightarrow B(k^{\frac{1}{n}})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq(8\frac{1}{36})^{\frac{n}{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⁤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>36</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq(8\frac{1}{36})^{\frac{n}{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We consider the following sequence of <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> potential embeddings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S5.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(1,\dots,1,k)\hookrightarrow E(k^{\frac{1}{n}},1,\dots,1,k^{\frac{n-1}{n}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(1,\dots,1,k)\hookrightarrow E(k^{\frac{1}{n}},1,\dots,1,k^{\frac{n-1}{n}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S5.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hookrightarrow E(k^{\frac{1}{n}},k^{\frac{1}{n}},1,\dots,1,k^{\frac{n-2}{n}})\hookrightarrow\dots\hookrightarrow E(k^{\frac{1}{n}},\dots,k^{\frac{1}{n}},1,k^{\frac{2}{n}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hookrightarrow E(k^{\frac{1}{n}},k^{\frac{1}{n}},1,\dots,1,k^{\frac{n-2}{n}})\hookrightarrow\dots\hookrightarrow E(k^{\frac{1}{n}},\dots,k^{\frac{1}{n}},1,k^{\frac{2}{n}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S5.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hookrightarrow E(k^{\frac{1}{n}},\dots,k^{\frac{1}{n}})=B(k^{\frac{1}{n}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hookrightarrow E(k^{\frac{1}{n}},\dots,k^{\frac{1}{n}})=B(k^{\frac{1}{n}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The first <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-2</annotation></semantics></math> embeddings will exist by Theorems <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 3.2. ‣ Proof. ‣ 3 Volume filling embeddings ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> and Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Proposition 3.4. ‣ Proof. ‣ 3 Volume filling embeddings ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> provided we have</p>
<table id="S5.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\frac{n-i}{n}}\geq\frac{(5k^{\frac{n-i-2}{n}}+16)^{2}}{16k^{\frac{n-i-2}{n}}}=\frac{25}{16}k^{\frac{n-i-2}{n}}+10+16k^{\frac{-(n-i-2)}{n}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>25</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\frac{n-i}{n}}\geq\frac{(5k^{\frac{n-i-2}{n}}+16)^{2}}{16k^{\frac{n-i-2}{n}}}=\frac{25}{16}k^{\frac{n-i-2}{n}}+10+16k^{\frac{-(n-i-2)}{n}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">for all <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq i\leq n-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq i\leq n-3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For the final embedding, as the target is a ball, we get an improved bound by appealing directly to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, Theorem <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.1.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.1.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.1.2</annotation></semantics></math> (iv). This says that <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(1,k^{\frac{2}{n}})\hookrightarrow B(k^{\frac{1}{n}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(1,k^{\frac{2}{n}})\hookrightarrow B(k^{\frac{1}{n}})</annotation></semantics></math> whenever <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\frac{2}{n}}\geq 8\frac{1}{36}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⁤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>36</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\frac{2}{n}}\geq 8\frac{1}{36}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore by Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Proposition 3.4. ‣ Proof. ‣ 3 Volume filling embeddings ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> the final embedding will also exist provided <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\frac{2}{n}}\geq 8\frac{1}{36}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⁤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>36</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\frac{2}{n}}\geq 8\frac{1}{36}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For the first <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-2</annotation></semantics></math> embeddings we require</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S5.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{25}{16}k^{\frac{-2}{n}}+10k^{\frac{-(n-i)}{n}}+16k^{\frac{-2(n-i-1)}{n}}\leq 1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>25</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{25}{16}k^{\frac{-2}{n}}+10k^{\frac{-(n-i)}{n}}+16k^{\frac{-2(n-i-1)}{n}}\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">for all <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq i\leq n-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq i\leq n-3</annotation></semantics></math>. The left hand side is an increasing function of <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, so it suffices that</p>
<table id="S5.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{25}{16}k^{\frac{-2}{n}}+10k^{\frac{-3}{n}}+16k^{\frac{-4}{n}}\leq 1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>25</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{25}{16}k^{\frac{-2}{n}}+10k^{\frac{-3}{n}}+16k^{\frac{-4}{n}}\leq 1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p8.2" class="ltx_p">A calculation shows that this also holds when <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\frac{2}{n}}\geq 8\frac{1}{36}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⁤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>36</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\frac{2}{n}}\geq 8\frac{1}{36}</annotation></semantics></math> and so Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a> <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i)</annotation></semantics></math> is proved. ∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">There is in fact a better bound valid in dimension <math id="S5.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{{\rm stab}}({\mathbb{C}}P^{3})\leq 21" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>stab</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>21</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{{\rm stab}}({\mathbb{C}}P^{3})\leq 21</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Part <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i)</annotation></semantics></math> of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a> already implies that <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{{\rm stab}}({\mathbb{C}}P^{3})\leq 23." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>stab</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>23</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{{\rm stab}}({\mathbb{C}}P^{3})\leq 23.</annotation></semantics></math>
Thus we just need to check that the same embeddings exist when <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=21" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>21</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=21</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>22</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=22</annotation></semantics></math>. Direct computation shows that the inequality <a href="#S5.E8" title="In 5.1 ℂ⁢𝑃^𝑛 ‣ 5 Examples ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> continues to hold for these <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, so we are reduced to showing <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(1,k^{\frac{2}{3}})\hookrightarrow B(k^{\frac{1}{3}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(1,k^{\frac{2}{3}})\hookrightarrow B(k^{\frac{1}{3}})</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=21,22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>21</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>22</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=21,22</annotation></semantics></math>.
We appeal again to McDuff-Schlenk’s results from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, Theorem <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.2.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5.2.3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.2.3</annotation></semantics></math> and its corresponding Table <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.2</annotation></semantics></math>. This says that <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}(\alpha)=\sqrt{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mi>α</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}(\alpha)=\sqrt{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> on the interval <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[7\frac{1}{9},8]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⁤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>9</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[7\frac{1}{9},8]</annotation></semantics></math> with the exception of eight intervals that are explicitly provided in their statement. The numbers <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\frac{2}{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\frac{2}{3}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=21,22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>21</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>22</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=21,22</annotation></semantics></math> do not lie in these intervals, thus this embedding also exists and hence the whole sequence of embeddings provided in the proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span><math id="S5.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first make the following observation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fact.</span> <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> embeds as a polarizing hypersurface in <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n+1}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n+1}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> Poincaré dual to the symplectic form.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Indeed, <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n}_{d}=H^{n+1}_{d}\cap{\mathbb{C}}P^{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n}_{d}=H^{n+1}_{d}\cap{\mathbb{C}}P^{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n+1}_{d}\subset{\mathbb{C}}P^{n+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n+1}_{d}\subset{\mathbb{C}}P^{n+2}</annotation></semantics></math> and we think of <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> as a hyperplane intersecting <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n+1}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n+1}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> transversely.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Now, <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is a curve of degree <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and so is fully filled by <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2}(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2}(d)</annotation></semantics></math>, normalizing the Fubini-Study form on the <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> so that lines have area <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, combining the above fact with Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 4.4 (Lemma 2.1, []). ‣ 4 Full filling by an ellipsoid ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> and arguing by induction we see that <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(1,\dots,1,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(1,\dots,1,d)</annotation></semantics></math> fully fills <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, arguing as in case <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i)</annotation></semantics></math> by applying Lemma <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.1</annotation></semantics></math> of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, to demonstrate Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a> <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ii)</annotation></semantics></math> it suffices to show that</p>
<table id="S5.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(1,\dots,1,k)\hookrightarrow\frac{k^{\frac{1}{n}}}{d^{\frac{1}{n}}}E(1,\dots,1,d)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><msup><mi>d</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(1,\dots,1,k)\hookrightarrow\frac{k^{\frac{1}{n}}}{d^{\frac{1}{n}}}E(1,\dots,1,d)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">for all integers <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq(\frac{25}{16}d+10d^{-\frac{(n-2)}{n}}+16d^{-\frac{2(n-1)}{n}})^{\frac{n}{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>25</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq(\frac{25}{16}d+10d^{-\frac{(n-2)}{n}}+16d^{-\frac{2(n-1)}{n}})^{\frac{n}{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We now consider the sequence of <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math> potential embeddings</p>
<table id="S5.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(1,\dots,1,k)\hookrightarrow E(\frac{k^{\frac{1}{n}}}{d^{\frac{1}{n}}},1,\dots,1,k^{\frac{n-1}{n}}d^{\frac{1}{n}})\hookrightarrow" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><msup><mi>d</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(1,\dots,1,k)\hookrightarrow E(\frac{k^{\frac{1}{n}}}{d^{\frac{1}{n}}},1,\dots,1,k^{\frac{n-1}{n}}d^{\frac{1}{n}})\hookrightarrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S5.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\frac{k^{\frac{1}{n}}}{d^{\frac{1}{n}}},\frac{k^{\frac{1}{n}}}{d^{\frac{1}{n}}},1,\dots,1,k^{\frac{n-2}{n}}d^{\frac{2}{n}})\hookrightarrow\dots\hookrightarrow E(\frac{k^{\frac{1}{n}}}{d^{\frac{1}{n}}},\dots,\frac{k^{\frac{1}{n}}}{d^{\frac{1}{n}}},k^{\frac{1}{n}}d^{\frac{n-1}{n}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><msup><mi>d</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><msup><mi>d</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><msup><mi>d</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><msup><mi>d</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\frac{k^{\frac{1}{n}}}{d^{\frac{1}{n}}},\frac{k^{\frac{1}{n}}}{d^{\frac{1}{n}}},1,\dots,1,k^{\frac{n-2}{n}}d^{\frac{2}{n}})\hookrightarrow\dots\hookrightarrow E(\frac{k^{\frac{1}{n}}}{d^{\frac{1}{n}}},\dots,\frac{k^{\frac{1}{n}}}{d^{\frac{1}{n}}},k^{\frac{1}{n}}d^{\frac{n-1}{n}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S5.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=\frac{k^{\frac{1}{n}}}{d^{\frac{1}{n}}}E(1,\dots,1,d)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><msup><mi>d</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=\frac{k^{\frac{1}{n}}}{d^{\frac{1}{n}}}E(1,\dots,1,d).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">By Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Proposition 3.4. ‣ Proof. ‣ 3 Volume filling embeddings ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> the <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i+1)</annotation></semantics></math>st embedding exists provided there exists an embedding</p>
<table id="S5.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(1,k^{\frac{n-i}{n}}d^{\frac{i}{n}})\hookrightarrow E(\frac{k^{\frac{1}{n}}}{d^{\frac{1}{n}}},k^{\frac{n-i-1}{n}}d^{\frac{i+1}{n}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mfrac><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><msup><mi>d</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(1,k^{\frac{n-i}{n}}d^{\frac{i}{n}})\hookrightarrow E(\frac{k^{\frac{1}{n}}}{d^{\frac{1}{n}}},k^{\frac{n-i-1}{n}}d^{\frac{i+1}{n}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p">which by Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 3.2. ‣ Proof. ‣ 3 Volume filling embeddings ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> exists provided</p>
<table id="S5.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\frac{n-i}{n}}d^{\frac{i}{n}}\geq\frac{25}{16}k^{\frac{n-i-2}{n}}d^{\frac{i+2}{n}}+10+\frac{16}{k^{\frac{n-i-2}{n}}d^{\frac{i+2}{n}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mfrac><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>25</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>16</mn><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\frac{n-i}{n}}d^{\frac{i}{n}}\geq\frac{25}{16}k^{\frac{n-i-2}{n}}d^{\frac{i+2}{n}}+10+\frac{16}{k^{\frac{n-i-2}{n}}d^{\frac{i+2}{n}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p7.3" class="ltx_p">or equivalently</p>
<table id="S5.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{25}{16}k^{\frac{-2}{n}}d^{\frac{i+2}{n}}+10k^{-\frac{(n-i)}{n}}d^{\frac{-i}{n}}+16k^{-2\frac{(n-i-1)}{n}}d^{-2\frac{(i+1)}{n}}\leq 1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>25</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{25}{16}k^{\frac{-2}{n}}d^{\frac{i+2}{n}}+10k^{-\frac{(n-i)}{n}}d^{\frac{-i}{n}}+16k^{-2\frac{(n-i-1)}{n}}d^{-2\frac{(i+1)}{n}}\leq 1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p7.4" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq d</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> fixed, the left hand side of this last inequality is an increasing function of <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore it suffices to check it when <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=n-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=n-2</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case we get</p>
<table id="S5.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{25}{16}k^{\frac{-2}{n}}d+10k^{\frac{-2}{n}}d^{-\frac{(n-2)}{n}}+16k^{\frac{-2}{n}}d^{-2\frac{(n-1)}{n}}\leq 1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>25</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{25}{16}k^{\frac{-2}{n}}d+10k^{\frac{-2}{n}}d^{-\frac{(n-2)}{n}}+16k^{\frac{-2}{n}}d^{-2\frac{(n-1)}{n}}\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p7.5" class="ltx_p">which is equivalent to</p>
<table id="S5.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\frac{2}{n}}\geq\frac{25}{16}d+10d^{-\frac{(n-2)}{n}}+16d^{-\frac{2(n-1)}{n}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>25</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\frac{2}{n}}\geq\frac{25}{16}d+10d^{-\frac{(n-2)}{n}}+16d^{-\frac{2(n-1)}{n}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p7.6" class="ltx_p">and so holds in our range as required.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Still following our methods here as well as those from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, the estimates for the upper bound on the stability numbers presented here may be improved if we can improve, by a much more complicated number theoretical investigation of the ECH vectors, the four dimensional embedding result from Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Ellipsoid embeddings and symplectic packing stability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>. However, in the case when the target manifold is <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> the inequality <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{stab}\leq 21" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>21</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{stab}\leq 21</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be improved in this way as it relies at the last step on a sharp estimate from McDuff- Schlenk giving the precise range for volume filling ellipsoids in the unit ball. It remains a very interesting question to find methods to obstruct symplectic volume fillings of ellipsoids or disjoint balls into the unit six dimensional ball. As will be shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, it appears that obstructions arising from symplectic field theory that have been succesful in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> when targets were cylinders fail here.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="bib" class="ltx_bibliography">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_bibliography">References</h2>

<ul class="ltx_biblist">
      
<li id="bib.bib1" class="ltx_bibitem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_bibitem">[1]</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock"> D. Bauer, The generating function for the embedding capacity for 4-dimensional symplectic ellipsoids. arXiv:1102.5630.

</span></li>
      
<li id="bib.bib2" class="ltx_bibitem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_bibitem">[2]</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock"> P. Biran, Lagrangian barriers and symplectic embeddings, <span id="bib.bib2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Geom. Funct. Anal.</span>, 11(2001), 407-464.

